<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Édouard Brémond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Édouard Brémond was a French army officer. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he led the French military mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, which supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a>, and from 1919 to 1920 he was chief administrator of the French-occupied zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, relying partly on the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>. Turkish nationalist resistance forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to agree to leave the region in 1921.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Brémond was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 5 November 1868 and graduated from the École spéciale militaire de Saint-Cyr in 1890. He served with the 1st Regiment of Algerian tirailleurs in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> from 1895 and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, where he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-hova-war">Franco-Hova Wars</a>. After studying at the École supérieure de guerre from 1899 to 1901, he joined the staff of the Constantine division, with duties in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a>, and from 1904 served with the 2nd regiment of tirailleurs, for which he set up a post at Sidi Bou Djenane.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Much of his early career was spent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. He was responsible for the Moroccan ports police in 1907 and 1908, deputy head of the French military mission there in 1909 and administrator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> and its suburbs in 1912 and 1913. He then headed the intelligence service of the Henrys column against the Beni Mtir and commanded at Kenitra. In August 1914 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to command the 64th Infantry Regiment and was wounded on the front.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> wished to assert its influence during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> and chose Brémond to lead its military mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 1 September 1916 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> on the 20th, and advised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> forces in sabotaging the Hejaz railway, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a>, his English rival, tried to undermine his influence. From 1 January 1919 to September 1920 he was Chief Administrator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> in the Occupied Enemy Territory Administration. He later wrote that the decision to withdraw from Marash remained a mystery, and that the order would not have been given had a wireless set allowed steady contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Brémond went on to command the 54th Infantry Regiment at Compiègne and ended his career as a brigadier general at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, commanding the 2nd group of subdivisions of the 17th region from 1923 to 1928. He was made a Grand Officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 22 November 1948, aged 80. He is remembered as a contemporary of Lawrence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and for his memoirs, La Cilicie en 1919-1920 (1921) and Le Hedjaz dans la guerre mondiale (1931).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Édouard Brémond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>France governed through military administrators such as Colonel <b>Edouard Bremond</b>, relying partly on the Armenian Legion.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cilicia">French Cilicia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in 10ᵗʰ arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in 15th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.88,2.36&amp;year=1920" aria-label="10ᵗʰ arrondissement of Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Édouard Brémond"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5133 5943h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5133 5943h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.88,2.36&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
